.TH GIT-PR-WATCH 1 "2026-10-15" "git-tools" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
git-pr-watch \- report whether GitHub pull requests are green, merged, or need action
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git pr-watch
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ number|url|branch " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git pr-watch
reads one or more GitHub pull requests and reports, for each, whether it is
done (checks green, or merged), needs action, or is still pending. Without a
target, it uses the pull request for the current branch. A branch target
resolves to this repository's open pull request for that head branch, or else
to its newest merged or closed one, so a branch keeps resolving after
auto-merge lands it. Pull requests from forks are watched by number or URL,
since anyone can open one with any branch name. Every target must belong to
the repository of the current checkout, and a run takes at most 20; requests
are pinned to that host and owner/repository, and ambient GH_REPO and GH_HOST
values are ignored.
.PP
All targets are read in batched GraphQL queries, at most eight pull requests
per query. When GitHub times out a query, it is split in half and retried.
.PP
A pull request is judged on its current head commit, and only that commit's
checks count:
.IP \(bu 2
The newest run of each workflow replaces older runs on the same commit.
.IP \(bu 2
A check counts as green only once its whole workflow run has finished, because
dependent jobs are created only after the jobs they wait on.
.IP \(bu 2
A failure while jobs created before it in the same run are still running is
reported at once. Any other failure or cancellation is reported once it has
stood for 150 seconds on GitHub's clock, so an automatic re-run or a replacing
run can take its place. A failure from an attempt that is being re-run is not
reported.
.IP \(bu 2
A workflow that failed to start, or that awaits a maintainer's approval, is
reported as such instead of as missing checks.
.IP \(bu 2
Right after a push, while GitHub still reports the previous head, the pull
request is pending.
.PP
When auto-merge is enabled, done means merged; a pull request that falls behind
its base then needs action, since auto-merge waits for it to be updated.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-wait
Keep checking until a pull request needs action, every pull request is done, or
the ones left are stalled (waiting on something outside the pull request). A
failure ends the wait as soon as it is final (see DESCRIPTION). Progress lines
go to standard error as states change. Run it in the background: a foreground
run can take hours.
.TP
.BI "\-\-timeout " seconds
With
.BR \-\-wait ,
stop after this long and report what is still pending. The default, and the
upper limit, is six hours (the longest a GitHub-hosted job runs), or
GIT_PR_WATCH_MAX_SECONDS.
.TP
.BI "\-\-interval " seconds
With
.BR \-\-wait ,
seconds between checks, plus a few seconds of random jitter. The default is
60 and the minimum 15.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help.
.SH WAITING
A one-shot run judges a single moment, so anything that needs time to settle
reports as pending. With
.BR \-\-wait ,
those windows pass: a pull request with no checks five minutes after the watch
started (or after its head last changed) stalls as
.BR no-checks ;
checks for another head for five minutes stall as
.BR head-mismatch ;
a merge state still BLOCKED on a second check, with every check finished,
stalls as
.BR blocked ;
a pull request that auto-merge should have merged but has not after ten
minutes stalls as
.BR merge-pending .
Once auto-merge has been seen enabled, a second check that finds it off while
the pull request is open needs action
.RB ( auto-merge-off ).
A new head mid-watch is followed: the watch announces it and starts that pull
request's windows over.
.PP
A rate limit is waited out. Each GitHub call is cut off after 60 seconds (less
near the deadline) and counts as a timeout; ten checks in a row that cannot be
read end the wait with status 3.
.SH OUTPUT
Standard output holds these lines, in this order:
.TP
.B pr=\fIn\fP state=\fIstate\fP reason=\fIreason\fP head=\fIoid\fP
One per pull request, in target order. \fIstate\fP is done, action, pending,
or stall; \fIhead\fP is the first 12 characters of the head commit, or
.BR \- .
.TP
.B check pr=\fIn\fP required=\fIyes|no\fP job=\fIid|-\fP name=\fIjson\fP
One per failing check of a pull request that needs action, at most ten per
pull request. The name is a JSON string and comes last.
.TP
.B check-more pr=\fIn\fP count=\fIk\fP
How many failing checks were not listed.
.TP
.B suite pr=\fIn\fP conclusion=\fIconclusion\fP workflow=\fIjson\fP
A workflow that failed to start.
.TP
.B run: gh run view \-\-job \fIid\fP \-\-log\-failed \-R \fIhost/owner/repo\fP
A command that prints a failed job's log.
.TP
.B action: pr=\fIn\fP \fItext\fP
What a stalled pull request is waiting for.
.TP
.B next: git pr-watch \-\-wait \fIn\fP...
The command to repeat for unfinished pull requests (exit statuses 1, 3, and 8).
.TP
.B result: \fIstatus\fP reason=\fIreason\fP prs=\fIn\fP,...
Always last: the exit status, the reason of the first pull request that
decided it, and every pull request that decided it, in target order. When the
run stops before every target is resolved, it lists the pull requests resolved
so far, and no
.B next:
line is printed.
.PP
Check names and workflow paths come from workflow files, which a pull request
author can write. Control and formatting characters are removed before
printing, but they remain untrusted text.
.PP
Pull request reasons:
.TP
.B done
green, merged, review-required, changes-requested
.TP
.B action
failed, cancelled, conflict, behind, closed, auto-merge-off
.TP
.B pending
checks-running, failure-confirming, push-grace, head-settling,
mergeable-unknown, merge-waiting, no-checks-grace, confirming, unreadable
.TP
.B stall
approval-required, too-many-checks, review-required, changes-requested,
no-checks, head-mismatch, blocked, merge-pending
.PP
Error reasons in the
.B result:
line: usage, not-found, auth, sso, graphql, environment, internal (status 2);
transient, rate-limited (status 3); signal.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every pull request is done.
.TP
.B 1
A pull request needs action.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, pull request not found, authentication or single sign-on
required, a query GitHub refused (for example, missing token scopes), local
environment problem, or internal error.
.TP
.B 3
GitHub could not be read: transient failures persisted, or a rate limit was
reached.
.TP
.B 8
Still pending, or waiting on something outside the pull request.
.TP
.B 128+\fIN\fP
Interrupted by signal \fIN\fP (INT, TERM, or HUP).
.PP
When several pull requests are given, the exit status is the first that
applies of 1, 3, 8, 0. Pull requests are read in batches; when a batch cannot
be read, the ones not read are reported as
.B pending
with reason
.BR unreadable ,
and a pull request already read that needs action still decides status 1.
.SH STATE FILES
So that other tools (agent hooks, for example) can tell whether a pull request
is being watched or has finished without asking GitHub,
.B git pr-watch
keeps small files under
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/git-tools/pr-watch/ ,
or
.I ~/.local/state/git-tools/pr-watch/
when
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
is unset or not an absolute path. With neither an absolute
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
nor an absolute
.BR HOME ,
there are none. This layout and its naming rules are a stable interface.
.PP
A pull request's
.I key
is
.IB owner _ repository _ number
and its repository's
.I scope
is
.IB owner _ repository ,
taken from the pull request's URL path
.RI ( /owner/repository/pull/number )
and lowercased, so they hold only lowercase letters, digits,
.BR . ,
.BR _ ,
and
.BR \- .
Compare keys and scopes whole rather than splitting them: a repository name
can contain underscores and dots, and some owner names (enterprise-managed
accounts) contain an underscore too. The host is not part of either: the same
owner, repository, and number on two GitHub hosts share a key, and so (very
rarely) do two repositories whose names differ only in where an underscore
splits owner from repository.
.TP
.BI watchers/ key . pid
Exists while a waiting run with that process id watches the pull request, and
holds the pid. The pid is the last
.BR . -separated
field and is all digits. A run killed outright leaves its file behind, so a
reader should also check that the process is alive and is
.B git-pr-watch
(the basename of its first or second argument, since it may run as
.BR "bash git-pr-watch" ;
where a launcher repeats the interpreter, as Termux does with
.RI \(dq "bash /path/to/bash git-pr-watch" \(dq,
skip a first argument whose basename the second repeats, then apply the
same rule to the rest).
.TP
.BI watchers/pending. scope . pid
Exists while a waiting run is still resolving its targets (branch names), and
stands for every pull request of that repository. The watcher files are
written before it is removed.
.TP
.BI exited/ key
Written when a waiting run ends, for each pull request it had resolved by
then, whatever its state, holding
.RI \(dq "status state reason epoch" \(dq:
the exit status, the state and reason last reported (or
.B \-
if not judged yet), and, when the run delivered a result for the pull
request (status 0, 1, or 8 with it read), when the check behind that result
started, in seconds since the epoch by this host's clock. A run cut short (a
signal, an error, GitHub unreadable) writes
.B \-
for the epoch, to be treated as before any event. An epoch later than an
event such as a push means the pull request was watched after it and its
result delivered, whatever the state. The file's modification time is when
the run ended; it is written before the watcher files are removed.
.TP
.BI done/ key
Written by any run that finds the pull request finished, holding
.RI \(dq "commit word epoch" \(dq:
the head commit judged (or
.B \-
if GitHub gave none),
.BR green ,
.BR merged ,
or
.BR closed ,
and when the check that judged it started, in seconds since the epoch by
this host's clock (or
.BR \- ,
to be treated as before any event).
Compare that time, not the file's modification time, with the time of a
later event such as a push: a check that started before the event cannot have
seen it.
.B green
is only written for checks judged on the pull request's current head. A run
rewrites the record only when its commit or word changes.
.PP
Names containing
.B ~
are temporary files (written as
.IB name ~ pid .XXXXXX
and renamed into place); readers ignore them. Files are replaced atomically,
and directories are created private to the user. Writing is best-effort: a
failure never changes the report or the exit status. A waiting run removes,
at its start, exit and done records older than 30 days, temporary files older
than an hour whose writer is gone, and watcher files older than a day whose
watcher is gone.
.PP
A reader that wants to know whether a pull request is watched should list
.I watchers/
before reading
.I exited/
or
.IR done/ ,
and list it once more if it finds nothing: a single directory listing can
miss both files while one replaces the other.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GIT_PR_WATCH_MAX_SECONDS
Upper limit, in seconds, on a waiting run (default 21600, six hours). A value
that is not a positive number is reported and ignored.
.TP
.B GIT_PR_WATCH_STATE
Set to 0 to keep no state files. Any value other than 0 or 1 is reported and
ignored.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Where state files go; see
.BR "STATE FILES" .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git-pr-checks (1),
.BR git-pr-ready (1)
